<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- INVITATIONS, the Universal Link half.
         The counterpart of the `autoVerify` App Link intent filter in `AndroidManifest.xml`, and
         it works the same way round: iOS fetches
         https://vinto.kupalinka.app/.well-known/apple-app-site-association and opens the app only
         if that file names this team and bundle id.

         THAT FILE IS NOT PUBLISHED YET (ship-and-operate 1.2), so today this entitlement changes
         nothing a player would notice — an https invitation still opens the website, which is a
         real page and a reasonable place to land. It is declared now rather than later because
         adding it is a change of *provisioning*: the App ID grows a capability, the profile is
         reissued, and doing that during a release is how a build stops signing on the afternoon
         it was meant to ship. The file can then be published without touching the app at all.

         `applinks:` only. This app claims no shared web credentials and no app-group storage. -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
    <array>
        <string>applinks:vinto.kupalinka.app</string>
    </array>

    <!-- THE COUNT OF THANKS, and the only thing this app keeps that outlives a reinstall.
         `NSUserDefaults` goes with a deleted app, so the number in `Thanks` — how many times
         somebody has said thanks — would be lost by a phone upgrade. Android needs nothing here:
         its vault file is already named in `backup_rules.xml`, so Auto Backup carries it.

         Neither store can rebuild this number, which is why it is kept at all rather than asked
         for. A consumable that has been finished is gone from the receipt, exactly as a consumed
         purchase is gone from Play's `queryPurchasesAsync`.

         KEY-VALUE ONLY. No document storage, no CloudKit container — this is one small integer,
         and `NSUbiquitousKeyValueStore` is the API Apple documents for exactly that.

         THE CAPABILITY IS NOT ENABLED IN THE PORTAL YET, and this entitlement changes nothing
         until it is: `Enduring.ios.kt` reads and writes nothing when the store is unavailable or
         the player is not signed into iCloud, and the count falls back to the device's own copy.
         Declared now for the same reason `associated-domains` above was — adding a capability
         reissues the provisioning profile, and doing that during a release is how a build stops
         signing on the afternoon it was meant to ship. -->
    <key>com.apple.developer.ubiquity-kvstore-identifier</key>
    <string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)$(CFBundleIdentifier)</string>
</dict>
</plist>
